\section{Propulsion System Derivation}
\label{sec:propulsion-system-derivation}

\subsection{Overview}
The UAP-01 Uranus Orbiter \& Atmospheric Probe employs a high-efficiency
ion propulsion system to provide the majority of mission $\Delta v$ for
deep-space cruise and Uranus orbital insertion. This chapter derives the
core propulsion relations, mass flow requirements, and performance
constraints consistent with the provisional mission $\Delta v$ of
18.5~km/s.

\subsection{Fundamental Relations}
Ion propulsion performance is characterized by:
\[
I_{\text{sp}} = \frac{v_e}{g_0},
\]
where:
\[
I_{\text{sp}} = \text{specific impulse}, \qquad
v_e = \text{effective exhaust velocity}, \qquad
g_0 = 9.81~\mathrm{m/s^2}.
\]

The Tsiolkovsky rocket equation:
\[
\Delta v = v_e \ln\left(\frac{m_0}{m_f}\right),
\]
with:
\[
m_0 = \text{initial mass}, \qquad
m_f = \text{final mass}.
\]

\subsection{Ion Propulsion Parameters}
Assume:
\[
I_{\text{sp}} \approx 3500~\mathrm{s},
\]
thus:
\[
v_e = I_{\text{sp}} g_0
\approx 3500 \times 9.81
\approx 34{,}335~\mathrm{m/s}.
\]

For a cruise and insertion $\Delta v$:
\[
\Delta v_{\text{ion}} = \Delta v_{\text{cruise}} + \Delta v_{\text{insert}}
= 5.31 + 10.47
= 15.78~\mathrm{km/s}
= 15{,}780~\mathrm{m/s}.
\]

\subsection{Mass Ratio Derivation}
From Tsiolkovsky:
\[
\Delta v_{\text{ion}} = v_e \ln\left(\frac{m_0}{m_f}\right),
\]
so:
\[
\ln\left(\frac{m_0}{m_f}\right)
= \frac{\Delta v_{\text{ion}}}{v_e}
= \frac{15{,}780}{34{,}335}
\approx 0.46,
\]
thus:
\[
\frac{m_0}{m_f} \approx e^{0.46} \approx 1.59.
\]

Propellant mass fraction:
\[
\frac{m_p}{m_0} = 1 - \frac{m_f}{m_0}
= 1 - \frac{1}{1.59}
\approx 0.37.
\]

\subsection{Mass Flow and Thrust}
Thrust:
\[
T = \dot{m} v_e,
\]
where $\dot{m}$ is mass flow rate.

For a representative thrust level:
\[
T \approx 0.25~\mathrm{N},
\]
then:
\[
\dot{m} = \frac{T}{v_e}
\approx \frac{0.25}{34{,}335}
\approx 7.28 \times 10^{-6}~\mathrm{kg/s}.
\]

Total burn time for ion-driven $\Delta v$:
\[
t_{\text{burn}} = \frac{m_p}{\dot{m}}.
\]

For $m_p \approx 1500~\mathrm{kg}$ (example xenon load):
\[
t_{\text{burn}} \approx \frac{1500}{7.28 \times 10^{-6}}
\approx 2.06 \times 10^{8}~\mathrm{s}
\approx 6.5~\mathrm{years},
\]
consistent with long-duration deep-space cruise and multi-pass orbital
insertion.

\subsection{Power Requirements}
Ion thrusters require electrical power $P$ related to thrust and exhaust
velocity by:
\[
P \approx \frac{T v_e}{2 \eta},
\]
where $\eta$ is overall efficiency.

Assume:
\[
\eta \approx 0.7,
\]
then:
\[
P \approx \frac{0.25 \times 34{,}335}{2 \times 0.7}
\approx \frac{8{,}584}{1.4}
\approx 6.13~\mathrm{kW}.
\]

This is consistent with a multi-thruster configuration powered by solar
arrays (inner cruise) and RTG/auxiliary systems (outer cruise).

\subsection{Distributed Thrust Architecture}
The UAP-01 propulsion subsystem employs:
\begin{itemize}
    \item multiple ion thruster heads,
    \item xenon tank cluster (x4),
    \item gimbaled thrust vectoring,
    \item cryogenic management for long-duration storage.
\end{itemize}

Total effective thrust:
\[
T_{\text{total}} = n T_{\text{single}},
\]
where $n$ is the number of active thrusters.

\subsection{Coupling to Trajectory}
The acceleration profile is:
\[
\mathbf{a}(t) = \frac{T_{\text{total}}}{m(t)} \hat{\mathbf{t}},
\]
and the trajectory is integrated via:
\[
\frac{d\mathbf{v}}{dt} = \mathbf{a}(t),
\qquad
\frac{d\mathbf{r}}{dt} = \mathbf{v}(t),
\]
subject to gravitational fields and UUAS-derived optimal sampling
constraints.

\subsection{Conclusion}
The UAP-01 ion propulsion system, with $I_{\text{sp}} \approx 3500~\mathrm{s}$
and exhaust velocity $v_e \approx 34{,}335~\mathrm{m/s}$, can deliver the
required $\Delta v_{\text{ion}} \approx 15.78~\mathrm{km/s}$ for deep-space
cruise and Uranus orbital insertion with a propellant mass fraction of
$\sim 37\%$ and multi-year thrusting. This derivation confirms the
feasibility of the propulsion architecture consistent with the mission
$\Delta v$ budget and trajectory design.